course: dictionary
lesson: 11476
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word affairs is a good example. «The minister spoke about world affairs on the news.» Here affairs means important matters that affect many people.
explain: The same word can also mean someone's own business and money. «Grandma asked the lawyer to help her put her affairs in order.» Her affairs are her money, her property and her plans for what happens to them. The clues are her and the words about money.
explain: Affairs can also mean a situation or an event. «The school trip was a noisy affair, with singing all the way.» Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Event fits. Then try your meaning in the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: Affairs is the plural of affair, so it often means more than one matter or piece of business.
fact: «Current affairs» means the important things happening in the world now.
fact: «Foreign affairs» means how one country deals with other countries.
fact: «State of affairs» means the situation at a particular time.
fact: «Mind your own affairs» means don't interfere with other people's business.
exercise: «The news talked about current affairs in Europe and Asia.» What does affairs mean here? | Shopping lists | Party games | *Important events and matters in the world | Lost property
exercise: «Grandad wrote down all his affairs so that his money was sorted out.» What are his affairs? | *His money and personal business | Clothes and shoes | Holiday photos | Football scores
exercise: «The picnic was a lovely affair, with cake and games.» What does affair mean here? | A cake shop | *An event | A shoe | A forest
test: Mum says, «Mind your own affairs!» What does she mean? | Please tidy your room | Go outside now | Tell me all your secrets | *Don't interfere with other people's business
test: Ella reads, «The state of affairs in the village was very calm.» What does state of affairs mean? | A kind of bird | A village map | A type of cake | *The situation
exam: Which phrase means the important things happening in the world now? | Mind your own affairs | *Current affairs | Foreign affairs | State of affairs
